{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-33035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point  $P(1,-\\sqrt{3},4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=8i+\\sqrt{27}j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=i-\\sqrt{3}j+4k$ and $n=8i+\\sqrt{27}j$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as \n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j) & =(i-\\sqrt{3}j+4k)\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j) \\\\ r\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-33035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j)=-1"] ["math" "r\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j)=8-\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "r\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j)=i-\\sqrt{3}j+4k"] ["math" "r\\cdot (i-\\sqrt{3}j+4k)=1"] ["math" "r\\cdot (i-\\sqrt{3}j+4k)=8i+\\sqrt{27}j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j)=-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $Q(3,2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,2,3⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨3,2,2⟩,$ $n=⟨2,2,3⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ & =⟨3,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-33312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩=16"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,3,3⟩=4"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,3,3⟩=⟨3,2,2⟩"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨3,2,2⟩=16"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨3,3,2⟩=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩=16"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $Q(1,2,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $n=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-33027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-5"] ["math" "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-9"] ["math" "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=-9"] ["math" "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=-9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point  $Q(3,2,0)$ and is  perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨1,-2,5⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨3,2,0⟩,$ and $n=⟨1,-2,5⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ & =⟨3,2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-33026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,5⟩=7"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩=-1"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩=⟨3,2,0⟩"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨3,2,0⟩=-4"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨3,2,0⟩=⟨-1,2,-5⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩=-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point  $Q(5,2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=6i+5j+4k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=5i+2j+3k$ and $n=6i+5j+4k$ is normal to the plane. \n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot (6i+5j+4k) & =(5i+2j+3k)\\cdot (6i+5j+4k) \\\\ r\\cdot (6i+5j+4k) & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-33322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot (5i+2j+3k)=25"] ["math" "r\\cdot (5i+2j+3k)=52"] ["math" "r\\cdot (6i+5j+4k)=0"] ["math" "r\\cdot (6i+5j+4k)=52"] ["math" "r\\cdot (6i+5j+4k)=5i"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-33322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot (6i+5j+4k)=52"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩=10.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩=10$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1}-2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =2{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{2} \\\\ 2{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-70814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,0,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨3,-3,1⟩=-12.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation of the plane, $r\\cdot ⟨3,-3,1⟩=-12,$  we get the normal vector $n=⟨3,-3,1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-3,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3{t}_{1}-3{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =3{t}_{2}-3{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},3{t}_{2}-3{t}_{1}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ eqauls $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,0,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{1}{-3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-70821" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70821" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70821" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,0,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨3,0,1⟩=5.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨3,0,1⟩=5$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨3,0,1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,0,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3{t}_{1}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =-3{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},-3{t}_{1}⟩\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-70817" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70817" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70817" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩=15.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩=15$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-2,2,-4⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ -2{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}-4{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{2} & =2{t}_{1}+4{t}_{3} \\\\ {t}_{2} & ={t}_{1}+2{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,{t}_{1}+2{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-70828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,b,\\,0⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,-3⟩=8.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,-3⟩=8$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-1,1,-3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,1,-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}-3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & ={t}_{1}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-70830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,3,5⟩-⟨-4,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨6,2,2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-127937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩=22"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,3,2⟩=14"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,6,2⟩=12"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨3,2,2⟩=8"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩=16"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩=16"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(-1,1,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5-3t \\\\ y=2+4t \\\\ z=-3+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-1,1,2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5-3t \\\\ y=2+4t \\\\ z=-3+t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨5,2,-3⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-3,4,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-3,4,1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-3,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ & =⟨-1,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-127944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩=9"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨-4,3,1⟩=7"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,3⟩=15"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,4,3⟩=11"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨3,1,4⟩=13"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩=9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(2,1,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(2,1,0)$ and $B(3,2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,1,-1⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,0)$ and $B(3,2,1)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,2,1⟩-⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨1,1,1⟩.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-70892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩=0"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩=2"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩=⟨2,1,-1⟩"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,1,-1⟩=2"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,1,-1⟩=⟨1,1,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩=2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(4,-2,3)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨2,-2,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-127956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot ⟨-2,2,1⟩=12"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,4⟩=22"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,-2⟩=8"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩=15"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨4,-2,1⟩=18"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-127956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩=15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(2,1,-3)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+t \\\\ y=2-t \\\\ z=3+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,1,-3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+t \\\\ y=2-t \\\\ z=3+2t\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨1,2,3⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨1,-1,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ & =⟨2,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfaac94bd0b4d595a377cd4441dbf9286c403902c2f3e6703575f61cf0f334c"} {"question" "q-70894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩=-5"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩=0"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨2,1,-3⟩"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩=-5"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨1,-1,2⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "q-70894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩=-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/state.json" "file_sha256" "f89099fbb3f4d5c4104681714cc1844cf2000836071f38d91c9b88970180b6c0"} {"question" "e-4932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(-3,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 0, 2, 0 \\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/example-4932.json" "file_sha256" "48f4f90a8b30b9d0b5794ce85853d58194d5a2a7b8ae3fde46d811c298cc0a79"} {"question" "e-4932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,2,0 \\rangle$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =⟨-3,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/example-4932.json" "file_sha256" "48f4f90a8b30b9d0b5794ce85853d58194d5a2a7b8ae3fde46d811c298cc0a79"} {"question" "e-4933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A nonzero vector $\\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c} \\rangle$ is parallel to the plane $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle  = 3.$ What is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/example-4933.json" "file_sha256" "e0c18de60d0e06c0e252ff35465a6df0a54d5129950caa3d996d2269f76cff45"} {"question" "e-4933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle  = 3$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle.$ So, $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-1,4⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+4{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =2{t}_{1}+4{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle t_1, 2t_1 + 4t_3, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_1,t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set $t_1=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any $t_3 \\neq 0.$ For example, setting $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{4}, \\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1} \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{b}{c} = 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/example-4933.json" "file_sha256" "e0c18de60d0e06c0e252ff35465a6df0a54d5129950caa3d996d2269f76cff45"} {"question" "e-4935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(\\sqrt{2},0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=-1+\\sqrt{3}t \\\\ z=5-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/example-4935.json" "file_sha256" "b912481a7fb121412248df876a4ba6cc934fc4ec1331f166c91dcccc0e13551d"} {"question" "e-4935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=\\langle \\sqrt{2},0,-5 \\rangle.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/assets/4c70e51d85874e5a241a5d9b35b147500d4c306187c5e0a1d15a48a9aef07706.png)\n\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1\\rangle,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2},0,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/example-4935.json" "file_sha256" "b912481a7fb121412248df876a4ba6cc934fc4ec1331f166c91dcccc0e13551d"}] "usage" {"q-70821" [] "e-4933" [] "e-4932" [] "e-4935" []} "basis" 1565 "retractions" [[17592186120435 :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨a, 0, c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r \\cdot ⟨3,-3, 1⟩ =-12$. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}$?"] [17592186119567 :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(-3,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 0, 2, 0 \\rangle$."] [17592186119567 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,2,0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nis normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf p$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n - p \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ &= ⟨ - 3, 2, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119568 :question/problem "A nonzero vector $\\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c} \\rangle$ is parallel to the plane $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle = 3$. What is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}$?"] [17592186119568 :question/worked-solution "We need a vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nthat is parallel to the given plane.\n\nFrom the equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle = 3\n$$\n\nof the plane, we get the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n⟨t_{1}, t_{2}, t_{3}⟩ \\cdot ⟨2,-1, 4⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n2t_{1} - t_{2} + 4t_{3} &= 0 \\\\\nt_{2} &= 2t_{1} + 4t_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle t_1, 2t_1 + 4t_3, t_3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere $t_1,t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0$. So, we must set $t_1=0$.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any\n\n$$\nt_3 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFor example, setting $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{4}, \\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1} \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{c} = 4\n$$"] [17592186119569 :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(\\sqrt{2},0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n${x = 3; y =-1 + \\sqrt{3}t; z = 5 - t$."] [17592186119569 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{q}=\\langle \\sqrt{2},0,-5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nConverting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{x = 3; y =-1 + \\sqrt{3}t; z = 5 - t\n$$\n\nThe direction vector of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 0, \\sqrt{3}, -1\\rangle\n$$\n\nso a normal vector to the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 0, \\sqrt{3}, -1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - q) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n - q \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= q \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{3},-1⟩ &= ⟨\\sqrt{2}, 0,-5⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{3},-1⟩ &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186119086 17592186119087 17592186119088 17592186119089 17592186119090] "immutable_fields" [[17592186119085 :answer-field/correct] [17592186119085 :answer-field/id] [17592186119085 :answer-field/key] [17592186119085 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
